  "prompt": "You are an associate analyst at Marshfield Croft Partners, working under Managing Director Lin Wei-chen in the firm's healthcare advisory group. UnitedHealth Group (UNH, CIK 0000731766) filed its most recent 8-K on May 11, 2026, and Lin has asked you to build a variance analysis workbook that will anchor our cost-overrun narrative for an upcoming client pitchbook. Your focus is UNH's Q1 2026 income statement and the controllable cost drivers pressuring operating margins.\n\nBuild the Excel workbook as follows:\n\n1. Pull Q1 2026 reported figures into a structured data sheet — Revenues of $111.721B, CostOfGoodsAndServicesSold of $12.823B, SellingGeneralAndAdministrativeExpense of $15.39B, OperatingIncomeLoss of $8.99B, and NetIncomeLoss of $6.28B — alongside prior-quarter and prior-year comparables sourced from the attached historical financials file (UNH_historical_financials.xlsx). All monetary values in USD millions, one decimal place.\n\n2. Build a variance calculation sheet with bridge tables that step from prior-period cost levels to current Q1 2026 levels, decomposing cost overruns into volume, price, and mix components where segment disclosures permit. Calculate gross margin variance (Revenues less CostOfGoodsAndServicesSold) and operating margin variance (OperatingIncomeLoss / Revenues), with a clear waterfall bridging the prior period to the current quarter.\n\n3. Express cost overruns as a percentage of Revenues. Flag any line item where the overrun exceeds 200 basis points relative to the trailing four-quarter average — label these as material deviations in red. Green-code favorable variances. Apply healthcare insurance sector-specific classifications, particularly medical loss ratio components, where UNH's segment data allows disaggregation.\n\n4. Link diluted EPS of $6.90/share back to the cost drivers in your variance decomposition. Present revenue growth rate, cost-to-revenue ratio change, and the EPS bridge on a summary dashboard tab formatted for direct pitchbook inclusion — clean enough for a non-technical board audience but with plain-language callout boxes explaining the key takeaways.\n\n5. Include a peer comparison stub tab with placeholder structure referencing sector benchmarks for cost-to-revenue ratios among healthcare insurers, and a notes tab documenting every assumption, data source, and any flagged anomalies (noting that the StockholdersEquity figure of $33.0B dates from June 2015 and should not be interpolated through the current period).\n\n6. Embed live formulas throughout rather than hardcoded values so the model supports scenario sensitivity. Every calculated cell must trace back to either a pinned fact from EDGAR filings or a clearly labeled assumption. Cross-reference trend consistency against UNH's historical filings and call out any one-time items that may be distorting the variance.\n\nThe workbook should be lightweight enough to perform cleanly in Excel 2016. Format for direct inclusion in the client pitchbook without further rework.",
